/* WHY THIS SPELLING AND NOT THE LOWER-SCORING ONE. A different statement
   order scores 8 DIFF and is the WRONG structure; read this before
   "improving" the number.

   The function is byte-identical to its twin func_800CE62C -- families.py
   reports the family of 2 with ZERO varying words -- so landing this lands
   both, 92 words.

   The 8-DIFF spelling is the same body in source order 0x2, 0x6, 0xA with two
   deltas hoisted into locals:

       d1 = -*(s16 *)((u8 *)b + 0xA);
       d2 = a->unk_86 - *(s16 *)((u8 *)b + 0x6);
       *(s16 *)((u8 *)b + 0x2) += (a->unk_84 - *(s16 *)((u8 *)b + 0x2)) / 2;
       *(s16 *)((u8 *)b + 0xA) += d1 / 2;
       *(s16 *)((u8 *)b + 0x6) += d2 / 2;

   It gets word 17 STRUCTURALLY wrong: it hoists a->unk_86 into the 4th
   post-call load slot where the target hoists b+6, and words 30/32 then carry
   the last subu with its operands reversed. The spelling in this file scores
   13 but has words 16, 17, 23, 28 and 30-39 EXACTLY right -- the whole b+0xA
   chain and the whole b+6 chain, including both hoisted loads the 8-DIFF
   version gets wrong. Its only faults are inside the first (b+2) chain, where
   every opcode and displacement is correct and only v0/v1 are swapped, which
   also pushes sh 0x2($s0) from word 25 to 29.

   Note the generated header above says VERDICT: SHAPE, not ALLOC, and that is
   the honest reading: advise.py sees the displaced store as a MISSING sh at
   800CE338/800CE344 plus an EXTRA sra at 800CE34C, and counts the 8
   register-only differences separately. The claim being made here is only
   that the shape fault is DOWNSTREAM of the register pairing -- see the
   local-alloc note below, where the target's word-26 srl reuses v0 and so
   forces the store to have happened already. If that reading is wrong, this
   is a shape park and the statement order is still in question. Positional
   DIFF is in any case the misleading number, as
   docs/near-misses/README.md warns.

   SEMANTICS, SETTLED. Three axes each move half the way to a target:

       if (--a->unk_6C <= 0) {
           func_800C4174(a, b, c);
           b2 += (a->unk_84 - b2) / 2;
           b6 += (a->unk_86 - b6) / 2;
           bA += -bA / 2;
       }

   with b2/b6/bA the s16 halves at b+0x2/+0x6/+0xA. The bA axis has no target
   field -- it decays toward zero. a->unk_6C is the s16 countdown, and
   lhu+addiu+sh+sll+bgtz is gcc's shape for `--x <= 0` on it. func_800C4174's
   three parameters pass straight through with no argument setup.

   THREE THINGS ESTABLISHED, worth not re-deriving:

   * STORES TO DISTINCT CONSTANT OFFSETS OFF ONE BASE DO NOT PIN SOURCE ORDER.
     memrefs_conflict_p (sched.c:614) proves them disjoint, so sched reorders
     them freely. The target emits sh 0x2, sh 0xA, sh 0x6 while this source
     order is 0x2, 0x6, 0xA. Sweeping all six permutations moved the score
     34 -> 13 and nothing else tried moved it at all. For the record:
     2A6=34, 26A=13, A26=49w/35, A62=49w/35, 62A=17, 6A2=47w/34. 62A is the
     exact mirror of 26A, which is what identified the first/last asymmetry.
   * A LOAD THROUGH THE OTHER POINTER IS PINNED; b-LOADS FLOAT. a->unk_84/86
     cannot be disambiguated from stores through b (distinct base pseudos, and
     the MEM_IN_STRUCT_P escape at sched.c:834 needs one address non-varying
     while both vary). So an a-load cannot cross a b-store while b-loads move
     freely. That is the machinery deciding word 17, and why every eager-delta
     spelling hoists a->unk_86 instead of b+6.
   * THE RESIDUE IS LOCAL-ALLOC ORDER, NOT THE STORE SLOT. In the target,
     chain 1 allocates a->unk_84 -> v1 and b+2 -> v0; the b+0xA chain's
     srl $v0,$a0,31 at word 26 then REQUIRES sh $v0,0x2($s0) to have already
     happened at 25, so the store slot FOLLOWS the allocation rather than
     causing it. This compile gives a->unk_84 -> v0 because in sched1's output
     that pseudo is born one insn earlier than b+2's and so sorts first in
     qty_order (local-alloc.c, qty_compare_1). Flipping that birth order is the
     open question and where a permuter run should be aimed.

   RULED OUT, each measured:

   * Assignment form: all 27 combinations of `x += e/2`, `x = e/2 + x` and
     `x = x + e/2` over the three statements -- all 13 in this order, since
     fold produces the same tree.
   * Operand-order rewrites of the a->unk_86 - b6 subtraction: -(b6 - a86),
     -b6 + a86, (0 - x), and a two-step d = a86; d -= b6 -- all 8. fold
     normalises every one to the same MINUS tree.
   * d = -b6; d += a->unk_86; -- combine DOES merge negu+addu into one subu
     and stays 46 words, but at 10 differing, worse.
   * Pre-read locals for b+2, b+6, a->unk_84 or both: the load is sunk back to
     its use and the inline score is reproduced every time.
   * Local types and declaration form: s16 (48-51 words, far worse), int, long,
     u32 with a cast, register, separate decls, reversed decl order, an extra
     unused local, initialisers in the declaration (47-50 words) -- no change.
   * Eager-delta grids: 2x2x2 in source order (best 8), 3x3x3 with
     inline/eager/pre-read (best 8), 2x2x2 in this order (best 13), and six
     split "delta early, accumulate last" arrangements (15-19).

   No permuter has been run on this function -- roughly 90 hand compiles only.
   The permuter is the right next step, aimed at the qty_order decision above. */

